# Target C-II — modern train slack forces either `0.373D` singleton trains or a rhombic-pentagon sea with quadratic Mantel deficit

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch; pentagon-interface consequences stated for `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8623`  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited modern train-slack theorem; independently audited singleton/rhombic stopped-train split; modern all-train pentagon sea  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_modern_pentagon_train_dichotomy_t18623.py`  
**Supersedes numerically:** the incidence bounds in `target_c_high_t_pentagon_rhombus_dichotomy_2026-09-15.md`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The modern high-`t` theorem gives the safe train-slack floor

\[
\liminf {\tau_D\over D}\ge \tau_0,
\qquad
\tau_0:=0.7467921.
\tag{MPT.1}
\]

The independently audited stopped-train bookkeeping identifies the two exact pieces of
this slack.  Let

- `S_D` be the number of singleton stopped trains, i.e. contact edges incident to no
  strict-rhombus face;
- `T_R=B_4/2` be the number of non-singleton stopped trains in the strict-rhombus
  complex;
- `R_4` be the number of strict unit-rhombus faces;
- `rho_rec=b+4f+Q-B_4` be unused recipient occurrence capacity; and
- `sigma_R=B_4-4sqrt(R_4)` be rhombic train-isoperimetric slack.

Then exactly

\[
\boxed{\rho_{rec}=2S_D,}
\tag{MPT.2}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{2\tau_D=\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R.}
\tag{MPT.3}
\]

Balancing the two terms at `tau_0` yields a concrete dichotomy.

### Branch S — singleton sea

\[
\boxed{
S_D\ge(0.373396-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MPT.4}
\]

### Branch R — rhombic train slack

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_R>(0.746792-o(1))D.
}
\tag{MPT.5}
\]

In Branch R the strict-rhombus stopped-train crossing graph is macroscopically far
from its Mantel endpoint:

\[
\boxed{
{T_R^2\over4}-R_4>(0.390-o(1))D^2.
}
\tag{MPT.6}
\]

The modern pentagon-sea theorem now makes Branch R much more geometric.  Throughout
`t<=1.8623`, Branch R has more than

\[
\boxed{1.244D-o(D)}
\tag{MPT.7}
\]

strict-rhombus/pentagon boundary incidences.  Consequently more than

\[
\boxed{0.622D-o(D)}
\tag{MPT.8}
\]

distinct non-singleton rhombic stopped trains terminate at a pentagon, and more than

\[
\boxed{0.248D-o(D)}
\tag{MPT.9}
\]

distinct pentagons have a strict-rhombus neighbor.  In fact more than `62%` of the
forced pentagons touch the strict-rhombus complex.

At the currently valid repaired O--O path endpoint `t<=1.8593`, the corresponding
figures strengthen to more than `1.281D` rhombus/pentagon incidences, `0.640D`
rhombic trains ending at pentagons, and `0.256D` rhombus-adjacent pentagons.  These
numbers do not use the path theorem; they are stated only to identify the overlap
window for later routing arguments.

No C-II contradiction is claimed.  Branch S still permits a linear singleton
population, and Branch R still permits locally near-degenerate pentagons.  The gain is
that the modern train slack is now represented by one of two explicit train networks,
not by an abstract recipient-currency defect.

---

## 1. Exact singleton/rhombic decomposition

The complete stopped-train decomposition includes a singleton train for each contact
edge incident to no strict-rhombus face.  Every non-singleton stopped train contains at
least one rhombus and has exactly two boundary occurrences of the strict-rhombus
complex.  Therefore

\[
T=T_R+S_D,
\qquad
B_4=2T_R.
\tag{MPT.10}
\]

The full endpoint handshake is

\[
2T=b+4f+Q.
\tag{MPT.11}
\]

Subtracting `B_4=2T_R` from (MPT.11) gives

\[
b+4f+Q-B_4
=2(T-T_R)
=2S_D,
\]

which proves (MPT.2).

The hostile-audited modern chamber theorem retains the exact older split

\[
2\tau_D
=(b+4f+Q-B_4)+(B_4-4\sqrt{R_4}),
\]

which is (MPT.3).

If `rho_rec>=tau_0D-o(D)`, (MPT.2) gives Branch S.  Otherwise (MPT.1) and
(MPT.3) give

\[
\sigma_R
=2\tau_D-\rho_{rec}
>(\tau_0-o(1))D,
\]

which gives Branch R.

---

## 2. Exact quadratic Mantel deficit in Branch R

Put

\[
M_D:={T_R^2\over4}-R_4.
\tag{MPT.12}
\]

The strict-rhombus stopped-train crossing graph is simple and triangle-free, so
`M_D>=0`.  More importantly, the definition of `sigma_R` gives

\[
\sigma_R=2T_R-4\sqrt{R_4},
\]

and hence exactly

\[
T_R=2\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}.
\tag{MPT.13}
\]

Substitution into (MPT.12) yields

\[
\boxed{
M_D
={\sigma_R\sqrt{R_4}\over2}
+{\sigma_R^2\over16}.
}
\tag{MPT.14}
\]

Canonical Type II has

\[
{R_4\over D^2}\to\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{MPT.15}
\]

Thus Branch R implies

\[
\liminf {M_D\over D^2}
\ge {\tau_0\sqrt{\Delta_2}\over2}
+{\tau_0^2\over16}.
\tag{MPT.16}
\]

The exact outward certificate evaluates the right side above

\[
0.3904459>0.390,
\]

proving (MPT.6).

This is substantially stronger than the weaker rationalized lower bound obtained by
dropping the `sigma_R^2/16` term and half of the linear term.

---

## 3. Modern pentagon sea at `t<=1.8623`

The modern all-train pentagon theorem proves

\[
\boxed{
{P_5\over D}>0.398175-o(1)
}
\tag{MPT.17}
\]

through `t<=1.8623`, and more precisely

\[
2{f\over D}-{Q\over D}
>0.398175-o(1).
\tag{MPT.18}
\]

Here `P_5` is the number of bounded pentagonal contact faces and

\[
Q=\sum_P(\deg P-4).
\]

The equality of the numerical lower expression in (MPT.18) with the pentagon bound is
not an extra hypothesis: degree arithmetic gives `P_5>=2f-Q`.

---

## 4. Low singleton waste forces pentagons onto the rhombic interface

Let `I_exc` count boundary occurrences having a strict rhombus on one side and a
bounded nonquadrilateral face on the other.  The total bounded exceptional-face
recipient capacity is

\[
4f+Q.
\]

The total unused recipient capacity over the outer face and all bounded exceptional
faces is `rho_rec`.  Charging every unused occurrence adversarially to the bounded
faces gives

\[
\boxed{I_{exc}\ge4f+Q-\rho_{rec}.}
\tag{MPT.19}
\]

Let `C_{6+}` be the total boundary-occurrence capacity of bounded exceptional faces of
degree at least six.  If `P_5` pentagons are present, then exactly

\[
C_{6+}=4(f-P_5)+(Q-P_5).
\tag{MPT.20}
\]

Since `P_5>=2f-Q`,

\[
\boxed{C_{6+}\le6(Q-f).}
\tag{MPT.21}
\]

Let `I_5` count strict-rhombus/pentagon boundary incidences.  Even if every available
boundary occurrence of every degree-`>=6` face is used against a rhombus,
(MPT.19)--(MPT.21) give

\[
\begin{aligned}
I_5
&\ge I_{exc}-C_{6+}\\
&\ge4f+Q-\rho_{rec}-6(Q-f)\\
&=5(2f-Q)-\rho_{rec}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{MPT.22}
\]

In Branch R,

\[
\rho_{rec}<\tau_0D+o(D).
\]

Combining this with (MPT.18),

\[
\liminf {I_5\over D}
\ge5(0.3981752\ldots)-0.7467921
>1.2440842.
\tag{MPT.23}
\]

This proves (MPT.7).

---

## 5. Convert interface occurrences to distinct trains and faces

Each incidence counted by `I_5` lies on a contact edge with a strict rhombus on one
side.  Therefore its stopped train is non-singleton and belongs to the strict-rhombus
train complex.  A complete non-singleton train has only two terminal occurrences, so
if `N_{R5}` counts distinct rhombic stopped trains having a pentagon endpoint,

\[
\boxed{N_{R5}\ge {I_5\over2}.}
\tag{MPT.24}
\]

Equation (MPT.23) gives

\[
\liminf {N_{R5}\over D}>0.6220421,
\]

proving (MPT.8).

A pentagon has only five boundary occurrences.  Thus if `P_5^{touch}` counts distinct
pentagons sharing at least one contact edge with a strict rhombus,

\[
\boxed{P_5^{touch}\ge {I_5\over5}.}
\tag{MPT.25}
\]

Consequently

\[
\liminf {P_5^{touch}\over D}>0.2488168,
\]

proving (MPT.9).

Dividing by the total pentagon lower scale from (MPT.17), the exact certificate also
gives the safe ratio

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {P_5^{touch}\over P_5}>0.62
}
\tag{MPT.26}
\]

in the adverse scalar envelope used here.

---

## 6. Overlap with the corrected live O--O path

The former path claims through `1.8623` were retracted because low projective
terminal energy did not by itself determine the oriented endpoint branches.  The
corrected opposite-branch theorem proves a live path only through

\[
1.844<t\le1.8593.
\]

At `T=1.8593`, the modern pentagon formula improves to

\[
P_5/D>0.405675-o(1).
\]

In Branch R, (MPT.22) then gives

\[
I_5/D>1.281584-o(1),
\]

so more than `0.640792D-o(D)` rhombic trains terminate at pentagons and more than
`0.256316D-o(D)` pentagons touch strict rhombi.

These are independent global populations.  This theorem does **not** assert that the
pentagon-ending rhombic trains lie on, cross, or terminate next to the selected O--O
path.  Establishing such routing is a separate closing problem.

---

## 7. Updated high-`t` branches

The surviving full-core train-measure branch now has the following exact structural
split throughout the modern range:

```text
S branch:
  >=0.373396 D singleton stopped trains;

R branch (for t<=1.8623):
  sigma_R >=0.746792 D;
  >0.390 D^2 strict-rhombus train Mantel deficit;
  >0.398 D pentagons globally;
  >1.244 D rhombus/pentagon endpoint incidences;
  >0.622 D non-singleton rhombic trains terminating at pentagons;
  >0.248 D distinct pentagons touching strict rhombi.
```

In the narrower valid O--O path band `t<=1.8593`, this split coexists with the repaired
common-hub path and its one-sided finite-strip consequence.  No such path statement is
used above `1.8593`.

The local pentagon regression still prevents a per-face metric contradiction.  The
next useful work is branch-specific:

1. in Branch S, exploit the `0.373D` singleton contact edges through their exact
   stopped-train directions, endpoint types, or one-edge flex coordinates; or
2. in Branch R, route the `0.622D` pentagon-ending rhombic trains through the phase
   structure and, in the overlap band, against the common-hub diameter intervals.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only modern singleton/rhombic-pentagon dichotomy with a
quadratic Mantel-deficit branch.  C-II and Level I remain open.
